---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

/**
 * Project deep-dive page for ROAR.
 *
 * Doubles as:
 *   1. The canonical "what I lead at Stanford" landing for site visitors who
 *      want depth on a specific project.
 *   2. The portfolio link submitted on job applications.
 *
 * Audience-agnostic on purpose. No specific recruiter or company is named.
 * Works whether the visitor is a hiring manager, a colleague, a partner, or
 * a curious stranger who clicked a link from LinkedIn.
 *
 * URL pattern: /roar (and future siblings like /pyafq, /cloudknot, /groupyr
 * for project deep-dives).
 */
---

<Layout
  title="ROAR — Adam Richie-Halford"
  description="ROAR is the open-source K-12 reading-assessment platform I lead engineering for at Stanford. Curated portfolio with videos, live demos, and engineering context."
>
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 sm:py-20">
    <header class="mb-10">
      <p class="text-sm font-mono text-accent mb-3 tracking-wide uppercase">
        Project · ROAR
      </p>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold text-slate-50 mb-4">
        ROAR — what I lead at Stanford
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        A curated walkthrough of the platform itself and the engineering work
        behind it: videos, live demos, and the parts I personally own.
      </p>
    </header>

    {/* What ROAR is. */}
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2
        class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 uppercase tracking-widest mb-4 pb-2 border-b border-slate-800"
      >
        About ROAR
      </h2>
      <div class="space-y-4 text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          ROAR is an academically-licensed platform for the assessment of
          foundational reading skills, developed by the Stanford Reading &amp;
          Dyslexia Research Program. It is browser-based, free to schools,
          available in English and Spanish, and integrated with Clever and
          ClassLink for single sign-on. Currently deployed in 309 K-12 school
          districts and 2,700+ schools, with 873K+ assessment runs to date.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Foundational Literacy suite — letter knowledge, phonological
          awareness, single word recognition, and sentence reading efficiency —
          fits inside a thirty-minute literacy block per student. Beyond that,
          we have a ROAR-Comp suite for reading comprehension, a ROAM suite for
          math, and assessments for visual attention and executive functioning.
          Score reports return same-day, broken down by skill area and
          color-coded by support level.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* Curated public materials. */}
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2
        class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 uppercase tracking-widest mb-6 pb-2 border-b border-slate-800"
      >
        See ROAR in action
      </h2>

      <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-100 mb-2">
            Try the assessments yourself
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Live demos of individual ROAR assessments. Free, no signup —{" "}
            <a
              href="https://roar.stanford.edu/#demo"
              class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
              roar.stanford.edu/#demo
            </a>
            . This is the most direct way to feel what a student feels.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-100 mb-2">
            How students experience ROAR
          </h3>
          <ul
            class="space-y-2 text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pl-5 list-disc marker:text-slate-600"
          >
            <li>
              <a
                href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XLLxKBJEeGc"
                class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                Guide to the Student Dashboard
              </a>: what a student sees on log in and during an assessment.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-100 mb-2">
            How educators use it
          </h3>
          <ul
            class="space-y-2 text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pl-5 list-disc marker:text-slate-600"
          >
            <li>
              <a
                href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zyDjHcUUUH8"
                class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                Guide to Logging In to Your Educator Account
              </a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a
                href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n8v6Cy6G7Tg"
                class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                Guide to the Group Score Report
              </a>: navigating classroom-, grade-, or district-level results.
            </li>
            <li>
              <a
                href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H1ALVWd4GLo"
                class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                Guide to the Individual Score Report
              </a>: interpreting one student's result and the recommended next
              steps.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-100 mb-2">
            The bigger picture
          </h3>
          <ul
            class="space-y-2 text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pl-5 list-disc marker:text-slate-600"
          >
            <li>
              <a
                href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mTyRmyBRHS0"
                class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                Introduction to ROAR
              </a>: the overview if you want one video to watch.
            </li>
            <li>
              <a
                href="https://youtu.be/cMAYb_E1qiI"
                class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                ROAR in Schools
              </a>: how districts and teachers actually use it day to day.
            </li>
            <li>
              <a
                href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uFk5GjE2ziQ"
                class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                ROAR: Breaking Barriers for Older Struggling Readers
              </a>: a specific use case for older students.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 pt-2">
          More on the platform and the underlying research at{" "}
          <a
            href="https://roar.stanford.edu"
            class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            roar.stanford.edu
          </a>
          ; the dashboard itself lives at{" "}
          <a
            href="https://roar.education"
            class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            roar.education
          </a>
          .
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* What I personally own. */}
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2
        class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 uppercase tracking-widest mb-4 pb-2 border-b border-slate-800"
      >
        What I lead
      </h2>
      <ul
        class="space-y-3 text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pl-5 list-disc marker:text-slate-600"
      >
        <li>
          <strong class="text-slate-100">The engineering organization.</strong>
          {" "}Built from one engineer to six, plus QA. I set the hiring bar,
          the operating cadence, and the standards for code review, release, and
          incident response.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-slate-100">Platform modernization.</strong>
          {" "}Leading the backend migration from Firestore to
          PostgreSQL/CloudSQL — data model redesign, schema work, staged rollout
          against active production traffic. Moving fragile research-era logic
          into explicit, testable services.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-slate-100"
            >Data governance and youth safety.</strong
          >
          {" "}Technical owner for FERPA-aligned audit logging and data
          lifecycle architecture, anchored to SOC 2 readiness. Accessibility
          (VPAT), least-privilege access, vendor security review, incident
          response.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-slate-100">Reliability foundations.</strong>
          {" "}The monitoring, error tracking, deployment, and on-call practices
          that took ROAR from research-cadence software to a platform districts
          schedule around for classroom use.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-slate-100">AI-accelerated team practice.</strong>
          {" "}Coding agents (Claude Code), AI code review on every PR,
          generative testing on highest-risk paths — backed by a ~70-page
          domain-knowledge document that grounds every AI-assisted change.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-400">
        More detail on any of these in the{" "}
        <a
          href="/resume/general"
          class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
        >
          short resume
        </a>
        {" "}or the{" "}
        <a
          href="/cv"
          class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
        >
          long-form CV
        </a>
        .
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* Honest framing. */}
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2
        class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 uppercase tracking-widest mb-4 pb-2 border-b border-slate-800"
      >
        On what you'd see today
      </h2>
      <p class="text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        ROAR is mid-migration on the backend (Firestore → PostgreSQL/CloudSQL),
        so the production build the videos and demos show is the version we're
        refactoring, not the version we'd ship today. That gap — the choices we
        made the first time and the ones we're making now — is the most
        interesting part of an engineering-leader conversation about ROAR. Happy
        to walk through it.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* Cross-links. */}
    <section>
      <h2
        class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 uppercase tracking-widest mb-4 pb-2 border-b border-slate-800"
      >
        More
      </h2>
      <ul
        class="space-y-2 text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pl-5 list-disc marker:text-slate-600"
      >
        <li>
          How I think about engineering more broadly —{" "}
          <a
            href="/about"
            class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
          >
            about me
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          Other projects (pyAFQ, Cloudknot, Groupyr) —{" "}
          <a
            href="/projects"
            class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
          >
            projects
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          Resume —{" "}
          <a
            href="/resume/general"
            class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
          >
            short version
          </a>
          {" "}or{" "}
          <a
            href="/cv"
            class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors"
          >
            long-form CV
          </a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
